'''arrivance†''' (a̤-rī′vans), ''n.'' [< ''arrive'' + ''-ance.''] '''1.''' The act or fact of arriving; arrival.
{{smaller block|Its [an animal’s] sudden ''arrivance'' into growth and maturitie.{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., iii. 9.}}}}
'''2.''' Persons who arrive; arrivals collectively.
{{smaller block|<poem>For every minute is expectancy
Of more ''arrivance'' [''arrivancie'' in early eds.].</poem>{{right|''Shak.'' (ed. Leopold), Othello, ii. 1.}}}}

'''arrive''' (a̤-rīv′), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''arrived'', ppr. ''arriving.'' [< ME. ''ariven'', ''aryven'', < OF. ''ariver'', ''arriver'', F. ''arriver'' = Pr. ''aribar'', ''arivar'' = Sp. Pg. ''arribar'' = It. ''arrivare'', arrive, ''arripare'', come to shore, < ML. *''arribare'', *''arripare'', reach, come to shore, earlier ''adripare'', bring to shore, < L. ''ad'', to, + ''ripa'', shore, bank.] '''I.†''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To bring (a ship or its passengers) to shore; land.
{{smaller block|Some points of wind . . . may as soon Overturn as ''Arrive'' the ship.{{right|''W. Brough'', Sacr. Princ. (1659), p. 486. (''N. E. D.'')}}
When Fortune . . . had ''arrived'' me in the most joyful port.{{right|''G. Cavendish.''}}}}
'''2.''' To reach.
{{smaller block|Ere he ''arrive'' the happy isle.{{right|''Milton'', P. L., ii. 409.}}}}
'''3.''' To come to; happen to.
{{smaller block|Lest a worse woe ''arrive'' him.{{right|''Milton'', Civil Power.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To come to or reach a certain point in the course of travel: with ''at:'' as, we ''arrived at'' Havre-de-Grace.
{{smaller block|<poem>When ''at'' Collatium this false lord ''arrived'',
Well was he welcomed by the Roman dame.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 50.}}}}
'''2.''' To reach a point or stage by progressive advance; attain to a certain result or state:

